Zero the subs_t that update_subscriber() hands to presence.

update_subscriber() fills in seven fields of a stack subs_t and passes it
to presence's update_shtable() with REMOTE_TYPE. The rest of the struct is
whatever the stack held, and update_shtable() reads more than those seven:

	if(subs->received_time == 0)
		subs->received_time = (unsigned int)time(NULL);
	...
	if((type & REMOTE_TYPE) && s->received_time > subs->received_time)
		return 0;	/* LWW: skip a stale update */
	...
	s->remote_cseq = subs->remote_cseq;
	s->received_time = subs->received_time;

Since presence's last-write-wins check (b5cd0046f8) a non-zero garbage
received_time lower than the entry's skips the update as stale - the
reg-event subscription's expires in the dialog table is not refreshed -
and a higher one is stored in the entry, so later genuine updates look
stale in turn. remote_cseq is copied from the garbage either way. Seen
under valgrind memcheck as the S-CSCF built reg-event NOTIFYs
("Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised value(s)" at
update_shtable, origin the stack frame of update_subscriber).

new_subscriber() and free_subscriber() in the same file already memset
their subs_t; do the same here.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_usrloc_scscf/subscribe.c b/src/modules/ims_usrloc_scscf/subscribe.c
index fd7b28c769..7be7233805 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_usrloc_scscf/subscribe.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_usrloc_scscf/subscribe.c
@@ -386,6 +386,8 @@ int update_subscriber(impurecord_t *urec, reg_subscriber **_reg_subscriber,
 	subs_t subs;
 	unsigned int hash_code = 0;
 	reg_subscriber *rs = *_reg_subscriber;
+
+	memset(&subs, 0, sizeof(subs_t));
 	if(expires) {
 		rs->expires = *expires;
 	} else {
